  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
    "lesson_core_move": "Assign the weight $x(\\tau)$ to each shifted impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau)$ in the continuous-time decomposition so the impulse continuum can be passed through an LTI system by superposition.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the continuous-time sifting representation, a signal is rebuilt from shifted impulses indexed by $\\tau$. The expression $\\delta(t-\\tau)$ marks the impulse located at the time selected by $\\tau$, so each value of $\\tau$ names one impulse in the decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The weighting rule is local: the impulse at location $\\tau$ receives the signal value at that same location as its coefficient. For example, if $x(2)=5$, then the shifted impulse at $\\tau=2$ is weighted by 5, giving the component $5\\delta(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{component at }\\tau=\\tau_0:\\quad x(\\tau_0)\\delta(t-\\tau_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The symbol $\\tau_0$ means one selected location. First identify the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau_0)$, then evaluate the signal at that same location. The value $x(\\tau_0)$ is the weight, and the shift $t-\\tau_0$ gives the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This integral is the continuum version of adding all weighted shifted impulses. For every possible location $\\tau$, the decomposition uses the component $x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)$, and the integral collects all of those components to reconstruct $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This structure matters because an LTI system can later handle the weighted impulse continuum by superposition. For now, the move is only to match each shifted impulse $\\delta(t-\\tau)$ with its weight $x(\\tau)$, not to compute the system output yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching a Shifted Impulse to Its Signal Value",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(\\tau)=2\\tau+1$, match the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-3)$ to its signal value weight.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-3)$. Match it to the standard shifted-impulse form $\\delta(t-\\tau_0)$, where $\\tau_0$ is the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-3)=\\delta(t-\\tau_0)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\tau_0=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The weight is the signal value at that same location. Substitute $\\tau=3$ into $x(\\tau)=2\\tau+1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(3)=2(3)+1=7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-3)$ is matched with the scalar weight 7. For this step, stop at the weight; the full weighted impulse component comes next.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing a Single Weighted Impulse Component",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a signal with $x(\\tau)=4-\\tau$, write the weighted impulse component located at $\\tau=1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested impulse location $\\tau=1$. For one selected location $\\tau_0$, the weighted impulse component has the form $x(\\tau_0)\\delta(t-\\tau_0)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{component at }\\tau=\\tau_0:\\quad x(\\tau_0)\\delta(t-\\tau_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\tau_0=1$, so first evaluate the signal at that location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(1)=4-1=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Attach that value as the coefficient of the shifted impulse located at the same $\\tau$-value. Since the location is $1$, the impulse factor is $\\delta(t-1)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(1)\\delta(t-1)=3\\delta(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the weighted impulse component is $3\\delta(t-1)$. The sign inside the impulse agrees with the location: a component at $\\tau=1$ uses $t-1$, not $t+1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing Several Weighted Impulse Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Using $x(\\tau)=\\tau+3$, write the weighted impulse components at $\\tau=0$, $\\tau=1$, and $\\tau=2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the list of requested impulse locations: $\\tau=0$, $\\tau=1$, and $\\tau=2$. Apply the same single-component rule at each location: evaluate the signal value, then attach that value to the shifted impulse at the same location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{component at }\\tau=\\tau_0:\\quad x(\\tau_0)\\delta(t-\\tau_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First evaluate $x(\\tau)=\\tau+3$ at each requested location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}x(0)&=0+3=3\\\\x(1)&=1+3=4\\\\x(2)&=2+3=5\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now pair each weight with the shifted impulse centered at the same location. At $\\tau=0$, the shifted impulse is $\\delta(t-0)=\\delta(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\tau_0=0&:\\quad 3\\delta(t)\\\\\\tau_0=1&:\\quad 4\\delta(t-1)\\\\\\tau_0=2&:\\quad 5\\delta(t-2)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the weighted impulse components can be written as the finite sum",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\delta(t)+4\\delta(t-1)+5\\delta(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Each term uses the same rule: the coefficient is the signal value $x(\\tau_0)$, and the impulse shift $t-\\tau_0$ records the matching location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Weighted Impulse Decomposition",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Handling Zero and Negative Impulse Weights",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Using $x(\\tau)=\\tau-1$, write the weighted impulse components at $\\tau=0$, $\\tau=1$, and $\\tau=3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still a list of impulse locations, so use the same component rule at each location. The new issue is that some evaluated weights may be zero or negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{component at }\\tau=\\tau_0:\\quad x(\\tau_0)\\delta(t-\\tau_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate $x(\\tau)=\\tau-1$ at each requested location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}x(0)&=0-1=-1\\\\x(1)&=1-1=0\\\\x(3)&=3-1=2\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now attach each weight to the shifted impulse at the same location. A negative weight stays as a negative coefficient; it does not change the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\tau_0=0&:\\quad -1\\delta(t-0)=-\\delta(t)\\\\\\tau_0=1&:\\quad 0\\delta(t-1)=0\\\\\\tau_0=3&:\\quad 2\\delta(t-3)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The zero-weight component contributes nothing to the finite sum, so it can be omitted from the final expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-\\delta(t)+2\\delta(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the weighted impulse decomposition for the listed locations. The coefficient sign records the signal value, while the shift records the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
